  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(-4,2)$ and $(2,-1),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-1-2}{2-(-4)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x.$",
